  <h2>Corrigibility framework</h2>
  <p>
    A framework for designing AI systems whose decisions can be inspected, contested, and
    overridden by the people they affect. The framework distinguishes architectural
    corrigibility (deterministic boundaries imposed outside the model) from behavioral
    corrigibility (the model's cooperation with correction), and argues that only the
    first produces accountability.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Status:</strong> drafts forthcoming. Read the in-progress writing at
    <a href="https://anivar.net/corrigibility">anivar.net/corrigibility</a>. ArXiv DOIs
    will be added here once the papers are public.
  </p>

  <h2>Related work</h2>
  <p>
    The accord draws on several existing bodies of work, including:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The Open Source Initiative's
      <a href="https://opensource.org/ai/open-source-ai-definition">Open Source AI Definition</a>,
      which sets the baseline for openness across code, parameters, and training data
      information.
    </li>
    <li>
      Princeton's research on AI agent reliability and the gap between benchmark
      performance and real-world deployment.
    </li>
    <li>
      The emerging <em>Agentic Skills</em> specification work on composable, inspectable
      capabilities for agent systems.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    See the <a href="/references/">References</a> page for the full lineage and
